# Local Python 3 / modern-pysam compatibility patch for find_circ.
#
# Upstream: https://github.com/marvin-jens/find_circ
# Upstream commit: 8655dca54970fcf7e92e22fbf57e1188724dda7d (2017-02-28)
# Upstream file: find_circ.py
#
# sha256 of the GENUINE unmodified upstream file at that commit, fetched
#   directly from raw.githubusercontent.com (NOT from the server's local
#   "find_circ_orig.py" -- see the correction note below for why that
#   distinction matters):
#   b67a043971c1b8eaa1eb672a1079e2cfc192a0c45ad56124538e8169e67ee174
# Verify any freshly-fetched copy against this hash before applying this
# patch to it -- if it doesn't match, the upstream file has changed (or the
# fetch is corrupt) and this patch's hunks are not guaranteed to apply
# cleanly or correctly; stop and re-check rather than force-applying.
#
# sha256 of the PATCHED file actually running on the server
#   (/home3/choukaihsuan/tools/find_circ/find_circ.py, confirmed directly
#   on the server, not from a cached copy) -- verify the result of
#   applying this patch against THIS hash too:
#   28a3cb9e99a11a882c26bae250f45235dff3f3fc7594d427ea24dcf553434f00
# This second check is the one that matters most: it is what ties "the
# find_circ inside the built image" to "the exact file that produced the
# 19 published datasets" at the bit level, not just "same commit + a patch
# that looks right." A build pipeline wiring this in must hard-fail (not
# warn) on either check -- a warning in a build log is something nobody
# reads. containers/build_image_a_find_circ.sh implements both checks.
#
# CORRECTION (2026-10-31, same day this file was first added): the first
# version of this patch was generated against
# /home3/choukaihsuan/tools/find_circ/find_circ_orig.py on the server,
# trusting its name ("orig") to mean "genuine unmodified upstream." It does
# not: directly fetching commit 8655dca5 from GitHub and diffing it against
# that "orig" file turned up 8 further differences (Python 2 print
# statements already converted to print(), `izip_longest` already renamed
# to `zip_longest`, three dict `.values()`/`.items()` calls already wrapped
# in `list(...)`) that are NOT part of this patch's own changes -- they were
# already present in both find_circ_orig.py and find_circ.py, meaning
# find_circ_orig.py itself had already received a prior, separate Python 3
# pass (likely an automated `2to3`-style one) before anyone on this project
# took that file as a baseline. The first version of this patch, and the
# sha256 it recorded as "upstream," both silently inherited that
# pre-existing modification. Caught because
# containers/build_image_a_find_circ.sh's upstream sha256 check -- fetching
# the real commit fresh and hard-failing on mismatch -- did exactly its job
# on the first real run. This version is regenerated directly against the
# genuine upstream blob; the sha256 above and the diff below are both
# corrected. The resulting PATCHED file is unchanged (still
# 28a3cb9e...; this correction only affects what counts as "upstream" and
# "the patch," not the server's actual running file).
#
# License note: find_circ itself is GPLv3 (see its own LICENSE file) -- this
# repository is MIT. This patch is kept as a diff against the upstream file,
# never as a vendored copy of find_circ.py itself, specifically to avoid
# redistributing GPLv3 source inside an MIT repository. A build that needs
# find_circ fetches the upstream file at build time, verifies its sha256
# against the value above, and applies this patch to it -- see
# containers/build_notes.md for how this is wired into an actual build.
#
# What this patch does: upstream find_circ.py (2017) targets Python 2 and
# an old pysam API. Running it under Python 3 with a current pysam fails
# in multiple places; this patch is the minimal set of changes needed to
# get it running under the server's Python 3.7.12 + pysam 0.21.0, not a
# rewrite or a style cleanup:
#   - Python 2 `print` statements -> `print(...)` calls (3 call sites)
#   - `from itertools import izip_longest` -> `zip_longest` (renamed in
#     Python 3's itertools; one import site, one call site)
#   - three `dict.values()`/`dict.items()` call sites wrapped in `list(...)`
#     (Python 3's views can't be iterated-and-mutated the way the original
#     Python 2 code relied on in those loops)
#   - `file(...)` -> `open(...)` (file() was removed in Python 3)
#   - integer division `/` -> `//` where an index was expected (Python 3's
#     `/` always returns float)
#   - `.decode('string_escape')` removed (that codec doesn't exist in
#     Python 3; the value is used as-is)
#   - pysam API renames: `pysam.Samfile` -> `pysam.AlignmentFile`,
#     `.pos`/`.aend`/`.tid`/`.qname` -> `.reference_start`/`.reference_end`/
#     `.reference_id`/`.query_name`, `.getrname()` -> `.get_reference_name()`
#     (all renamed in a pysam API version well past what was current in 2017)
#   - `complement()`: strips stray `\n`/`\r` before translating, and falls
#     back to "N" for any character not in the COMPLEMENT table instead of
#     raising KeyError (the original crashed on any ambiguity-code base
#     sequence reads sometimes contain)
#   - `mismatches()`: replaced a `numpy.fromstring(..., dtype=byte)`-based
#     comparison (the `fromstring` ctor used this way is deprecated/removed
#     in modern numpy) with a plain Python `sum(x != y for x, y in zip(a,b))`,
#     also handling unequal-length inputs instead of letting the original
#     silently rely on numpy's elementwise broadcast
#   - module-level `import numpy as _np` added -- this is now DEAD CODE,
#     not a random leftover line: an intermediate (not the final) version
#     of `mismatches()` used `_np.frombuffer(...)` directly, and a crash
#     log on the server from that stage
#     (GSE55872_results/circRNA/SRR444655/find_circ/find_circ.log, dated
#     2026-06-04, the same day as this patch) shows exactly that call
#     failing with `NameError: name '_np' is not defined` before this
#     import was added. `mismatches()` was then rewritten again to the
#     plain-Python version actually in this diff, which never references
#     `_np` -- confirmed by grepping the full current find_circ.py on the
#     server: `_np` appears ONLY on this import line, nowhere else. Kept
#     as-is (not removed) because the server's actual running file has
#     it; removing it would change the sha256 this patch is asserted
#     against.
#
# Not touched: this patch does not change find_circ's detection algorithm,
# its CLI, or its output format/columns -- only what was necessary to make
# it import and run at all under Python 3. The triplicated
# `if A is None or B is None: break` block this diff shows (three identical
# lines in a row) is preserved exactly as it exists in the server's actual
# patched copy -- it is redundant but harmless (idempotent check), and this
# patch intentionally captures what is ACTUALLY running, not a cleaned-up
# version of it; CLAUDE.md's existing description of "three bugs fixed"
# undercounts what's in this diff by a wide margin -- this patch file is
# now the authoritative record, superseding that summary.
